From c4e4482746c27ec637d2aa7ec26e378918d81b66 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: shine5402 <14089306+shine5402@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 12:48:31 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] WebEngineView: a backgroundThrottling property

A hidden WebEngineView is throttled like a background tab. An
application that keeps a page working in the background, such as one
that drives playback from a page's timers, has no way to opt out.

Add a backgroundThrottling property to WebEngineView, true by default
and changeable at any time, after Electron's webPreferences option of
the same name and built the way Electron builds it, on the two Chromium
patches before this one. While it is false, a hidden page keeps running
as a shown one does: its timers run at full rate, and
document.visibilityState reports visible.

As Electron's WebContents::SetBackgroundThrottling and
HandleNewRenderFrame do, the setting goes to every frame of the page,
including frames created later: each frame's widget is kept from being
hidden, and each RenderView has scheduler throttling disallowed. The
page's own view is shown again if it was hidden, and hidden again when
throttling is allowed while the view is hidden. What Electron does in
RenderWidgetHostViewAura is done in RenderWidgetHostViewQt: a widget
kept from being hidden stays shown, attached to its compositor, when its
item is hidden. (After a navigation into a new process while the view
is hidden, the page's timers keep their full rate and it still reports
visible, but requestAnimationFrame callbacks stop until the view is
shown.)

A page with throttling off is also never recommended to be frozen. A
view that leaves the property at its default never reaches any of this.
